    36	#    - The separator color has repeated geometric support in every sample: two full
    37	#      rows and two full columns in the input motif, then four separator rows and
    38	#      four separator columns in the output, plus eight entire 3x3 empty slots.
    39	#    - This is visually stronger than plain D2/1, because the output no longer has
    40	#      isolated background holes inside the 19x19 expansion. The grid frame becomes
    41	#      an obvious object in its own right.
    42	#    - The eight real source blocks still carry asymmetric content constraints, so
    43	#      the task does not collapse into "fill everything with the separator color".
    44	#
    45	# c) Implicit assumptions from the original solver that break
    46	#    - `h % 3 == 0` is invalid because the input motif bbox is now 11x11, not 9x9.
    47	#    - `block = h // 3` ignores gutter pixels; block starts must use pitch 4.
    48	#    - Source cropping via `br * block` / `bc * block` is wrong; starts are
    49	#      `br * (BLOCK + SEP)` and `bc * (BLOCK + SEP)`.
    50	#    - The original center sampling offsets `[-1, 1, 3]` assumed the hole touched
    51	#      neighboring blocks directly. With 1-pixel gutters, the inward-facing samples
    52	#      shift to the three block-aligned rows/cols `[2, 5, 8]`.
    53	#    - Majority-color detection may be dominated by the separator color unless the
    54	#      separator is explicitly excluded.
    55	#    - After D13/0, the output can no longer be initialized as background with only
    56	#      separator lines drawn. The 8 blank logical slots must also be prefilled with
    57	#      separator color before the copied blocks are pasted.
    58	#
    59	# d) Placement legality
    60	#    - Input motif size is 11x11 and the expanded output region is 19x19, so the
    61	#      generator places the input motif with a 4-pixel margin on each side. Then
    62	#      pasting the 19x19 output at `(r0 - 4, c0 - 4)` is always in-bounds.
    63	#    - Only one motif is placed on the canvas, so there is no overlap ambiguity.
    64	#    - The separator color is chosen distinct from background and from all block
    65	#      colors, so the solver's separator parsing remains unambiguous.
    66	#
    67	# e) Visual observability
    68	#    - In 2-4 examples a human can see three stable cues:
    69	#      1. input has a colored lattice between the 3x3 blocks;
    70	#      2. output extends that lattice outward to a 5x5 logical array;
    71	#      3. the places where no copied block lands are not background holes anymore,
    72	#         but solid separator-colored 3x3 pads.
    73	#    - No step depends on raw color ids; the cue is geometric repetition.
    74	#
    75	# f) Visual cleanliness
